(committed to them they discharged very judiciously, by arranging the) Tj T*
(new portions in the order pointed out, without altering the text,) Tj T*
(and, except in a very few instances, without additions of their own,) Tj T*
(contenting themselves with adding some explanatory notes, consisting) Tj T*
(chiefly of references to the author?s other works. [p004]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Some of the chapters thus added are unfortunately mere fragments,) Tj T*
(but most of the others indicate very clearly Bastiat?s opinions on) Tj T*
(the subjects to which they relate, and several of them display a) Tj T*
(breadth, a vigour, and an originality worthy of the best days of) Tj T*
(their lamented author.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Many of the questions purely economical which are discussed in the) Tj T*
(posthumous portions of the work,?such, for instance, as those of) Tj T*
(Wages, Population, and the relations of Labour and Capital, etc.,?are) Tj T*
(still deeply engaging public attention in England, as well as on the) Tj T*
(other side of the Channel; and on subjects of such vast practical) Tj T*
(importance it is surely desirable that the opinions of so profound) Tj T*
(and fearless a thinker as Bastiat should be as widely disseminated as) Tj T*
(possible.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In conclusion, I may perhaps be permitted to refer to the great) Tj T*
(interest taken in this translation by the late Mr Cobden, who was the) Tj T*
(correspondent and personal friend of Bastiat, and was, I need not) Tj T*
(say, so eminently qualified to form and pronounce an opinion on the) Tj T*
(merits of his last great work. A short time after the appearance of) Tj T*
(the first ten chapters \(26th March 1860\), writing from Paris, where) Tj T*
(he was then engaged in negotiating the Commercial Treaty, Mr Cobden) Tj T*
(says, ?My enthusiasm for Bastiat, founded as much on a love of his) Tj T*
(personal qualities as on an admiration for his genius, dates back) Tj T*
(nearly twenty years. I need not, therefore, express any astonishment) Tj T*
(at the warmth with which you speak of his productions. They are doing) Tj T*
(their work silently but effectually. M. Guillaumin [the eminent) Tj T*
(publisher] tells me the sale of the last edition has been steady and) Tj T*
(continuous, and a new one is now in hand. The works of Bastiat, which) Tj T*
(are selling not only in France, but throughout Europe, are gradually) Tj T*
(teaching those who, by their commanding talents, are capable of) Tj T*
(becoming the teachers of others; for Bastiat speaks with the greatest) Tj T*
(force to the highest order of intellects. At the same time, he is) Tj T*
(almost the only political economist whose style is brilliant and) Tj T*
(fascinating, whilst his irresistible logic is [p005] relieved by) Tj T*
(sallies of wit and humour which make his ?Sophismes? as amusing as) Tj T*
(a novel. No critic who has read Bastiat will dare to apply again to) Tj T*
(Political Economy the sarcastic epithet of the ?dreary science.? His) Tj T*
(fame is so well established, that I think it would be presumptuous) Tj T*
(to do anything to increase it by any other means than the silent but) Tj T*
(certain dissemination of his works by the force of their own great) Tj T*
(merits.?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(A word as to my mode of rendering Bastiat. I have not aimed at) Tj T*
(giving a literal translation. Indeed, the language often employed by) Tj T*
(Bastiat hardly admits of literal translation. But the more important) Tj T*
