(«Frédéric Bastiat», whose last and greatest, though, alas! unfinished) Tj T*
(work?the ?Harmonies Économiques??I now venture to introduce to the) Tj T*
(English public, was born at Bayonne, on the 19th of June 1801. His) Tj T*
(father, an eminent merchant of Bayonne, died young, and his wife) Tj T*
(having died before him, Frédéric, their only child, was left an) Tj T*
(orphan at the early age of nine years.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The care of his education devolved on his paternal grandfather, who) Tj T*
(was proprietor of a land estate near Mugron, in the arrondissement of) Tj T*
(Saint-Sever. His aunt, Mademoiselle Justine Bastiat, acted towards) Tj T*
(him the part of a mother, and her affection was warmly reciprocated) Tj T*
(by Bastiat, who, to the day of his death, never ceased to regard her) Tj T*
(with filial love and reverence.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Bastiat?s education was begun at Bayonne, continued at Saint-Sever,) Tj T*
(and finished at the College of Sorèze. Here his course of study was) Tj T*
(occasionally interrupted by indisposition; but, on his recovery,) Tj T*
(his quick parts and steady application soon enabled him to overtake) Tj T*
(and keep pace with his fellow-students. At Sorèze. Bastiat formed) Tj T*
(a boyish friendship with M. Calmètes, to whom his earliest letters) Tj T*
(are addressed. The attachment of the youths was so remarkable, that) Tj T*
(the masters permitted them to prepare their exercises together, and) Tj T*
(sign them with their joint names. In this way they gained a prize for) Tj T*
(poetry. The prize was a gold medal, which, of course, could not be) Tj T*
(divided. ?Keep it,? said Bastiat to his friend: ?I am an orphan; you) Tj T*
(have both father and mother, and the medal of right falls to them.?) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In 1818, Bastiat left College, and, in compliance with the wishes) Tj T*
(of his family, entered his uncle?s counting-house at Bayonne. His) Tj T*
([p010] tastes, however, were for study rather than for business,) Tj T*
(and while at Bayonne he devoted his leisure hours by turns to) Tj T*
(French, English, and Italian literature. ?I aim at nothing less,? he) Tj T*
(said, ?than to become acquainted with politics, history, geography,) Tj T*
(mathematics, mechanics, natural history, botany, and four or five) Tj T*
(languages.? He was fond of music, sang agreeably, and played well on) Tj T*
(the violoncello.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In 1824, he began to study the works of the leading Economists of) Tj T*
(France and England?Adam Smith, Jean Baptiste Say, and Destutt de) Tj T*
(Tracy; and even at this early period he took an interest in the) Tj T*
(English free-trade measures of Mr Huskisson. From this time he may be) Tj T*
(said to have devoted his life to his favourite science.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(On the death of his grandfather, in 1825, he gave up commerce as) Tj T*
(a profession, and took up his residence on his paternal estate) Tj T*
(at Mugron, in the cultivation of which he was at first induced) Tj T*
(to engage, but without much success, and he soon relinquished) Tj T*
(agriculture, as he had before abandoned trade. Business, in truth,) Tj T*
(was not his vocation; he had no turn for details; he cared little) Tj T*
