(for money; his wants were few and simple; and he had no intention,) Tj T*
(as he says in one of his letters, to undergo irksome labour for) Tj T*
(three-fourths of his life to ensure for the remainder a useless) Tj T*
(superfluity.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(It was at this period, and at Mugron, that he formed his lifelong) Tj T*
(friendship with M. Felix Coudroy, to whom so much of his) Tj T*
(correspondence is addressed, and to whom, a short time before his) Tj T*
(death, he had thought of committing the task of finishing the) Tj T*
(second volume of the ?Harmonies?. The two friends, whose tastes and) Tj T*
(pursuits were the same, were constantly together,?reading, walking,) Tj T*
(or conversing. If Bastiat, whose ardent nature was impatient of) Tj T*
(plodding and systematic application, received a new book from Paris,) Tj T*
(he immediately carried it to Coudroy, who examined it, and noted the) Tj T*
(remarkable passages, which he read afterwards to his friend. Bastiat) Tj T*
(would often content himself with such fragments; and it was only when) Tj T*
(the book interested him deeply, that he would carry it off to read it) Tj T*
(carefully by himself. On these days, says his biographer, music was) Tj T*
(laid aside, and the violoncello was mute. It was thus, he continues,) Tj T*
(that the two friends passed their lives together, lodging a few paces) Tj T*
(from each other, seeing one another three times a-day, sometimes in) Tj T*
(their chambers, sometimes in long walks, sauntering together, book) Tj T*
(in hand. Works of philosophy, history, politics, religion, poetry,) Tj T*
(travels, biography, political economy, socialist [p011] works of) Tj T*
(the day,?all passed under the ordeal of this double intelligence. It) Tj T*
(was in these conversations that the ideas of Bastiat were developed,) Tj T*
(and his thoughts matured. When anything struck him particularly, he) Tj T*
(would set to work of a morning and put it into shape without effort.) Tj T*
(In this way he wrote his ?Sophismes?, his article on the French) Tj T*
(and English tariffs, etc. It was this literary friendship, which) Tj T*
(lasted for more than twenty years, without being once clouded by the) Tj T*
(slightest disagreement, which prepared the mind of Bastiat for the) Tj T*
(gigantic efforts he was destined afterwards to make, and enabled him,) Tj T*
(during the last five years of his life, amid disease and distraction,) Tj T*
(to give to the world that mass of original and varied ideas which) Tj T*
(compose the six volumes of his collected works.[2]) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(In the events to which the expulsion of the elder branch of the) Tj T*
(Bourbons gave rise in 1830, Bastiat took an active interest. Bayonne) Tj T*
(had pronounced in favour of the new order of things. The citadel) Tj T*
(alone held out, and continued to display the white flag; and a) Tj T*
(concentration of Spanish troops on the frontier was spoken of.) Tj T*
(Bastiat did not hesitate. Quitting Mugron, he hurried to Bayonne to) Tj T*
(take part in the movement. In conjunction with some of his friends,) Tj T*
(he prepared a proclamation, formed an association of six hundred) Tj T*
(determined young men, and did not despair of reducing the citadel by) Tj T*
(a ?coup de main?. Happily their martial ardour was not put to the) Tj T*
(proof. Before the march of events all resistance gave way, and that) Tj T*
(same day the citadel opened its gates. In place of a battle, there) Tj T*
(was a feast;?punch, wine, and Béranger enlivened the evening;?and) Tj T*
(the officers, like horses just let loose from the stable, were the) Tj T*
(merriest of the party.[3] Such was the beginning and the end of) Tj T*
